{
  "name": "vix_spikes",
  "ref": "repro:vix_spikes",
  "built": "2026-10-24",
  "claim": "Allocate more to short premium as VIX rises (sell when volatility is high)",
  "tasty_value": "Allocation skews higher in higher volatility (within 25-50% of buying power, max 75%); sell volatility when IV rank is above 50, especially with VIX elevated",
  "tasty_sources": [
    "ep:options-jive-2023-12-27@06:56",
    "page:concepts-iv-crush"
  ],
  "tasty_note": "The VIX-tiered allocation ladder is not verified from a primary tastylive source (Phase 03 needs-check).",
  "our_value": "After VIX first closes above 30 (n=54): VIX minus next-21d realized averaged +6.0 pts vs +4.1 on all days (positive 85% vs 85%); SPX +21d mean +2.2% vs +0.8%, up 74%; worst +21d -16.3%, worst intra-month low -24.9%, worst spread -55.5 pts; a >10% drop within 21 days followed 11% of events vs 4% of all days.",
  "data": "Yahoo ^VIX and ^GSPC daily closes",
  "period": "1990-01-02 to 2026-10-23",
  "method": "Events de-clustered 21 trading days apart. Forward SPX price returns at +5/+21/+63 trading days, VIX change, the lowest close in the next 21 days, and VIX minus the next 21 days' realized vol (zenith.beta.hedge.realized).",
  "caveats": [
    "Few independent events (dozens), dominated by 2008-09 and 2020; one more crash would move the averages.",
    "Price returns, no dividends; no option P&L: a positive average spread does not mean short options at the spike were safe (see the worst cases).",
    "Margin requirements rise with VIX, so a seller may be forced to cut size exactly here (lit:santaclara-saretto-2009)."
  ],
